                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "7be42d00-1767-4886-b0c9-0a4ad5574974",
                        "source_entry_id": "0be28502-867e-343f-9fd0-ca942dbeab39",
                        "source_sense_id": "5f43dc25-71a5-39d9-91b3-29311d4b3453",
                        "source_sense_description": "Botanik: das Fruchtgehäuse beziehungsweise die Fruchtwand bei Samenpflanzen",
                        "target_lang_word": "pericarp",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "pericarp",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "3c3a203a-5e70-3683-ab56-2292c98640a4",
                                        "entry_id": "53a6eb02-b29f-3fb4-a56c-117dc60d8176",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The outermost layer, or skin, of a ripe fruit or ovary."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "9218ff54-cd82-4df0-82ae-38c4d1604220",
                                                "sense_id": "3c3a203a-5e70-3683-ab56-2292c98640a4",
                                                "text": "When, as in the cherry, the different layers of the pericarp are well-defined and largely developed, they received distinctive names ; the outer skin is the epicarp (epi, upon, carpon, a fruit), the middle fleshy part is the mesocarp (mesos, middle) ; and the innermost hard shell is the endocarp (endos, within) ; in other words the pericarp is made up of epicarp, mesocarp, and endocarp. […] In all normal fruits these three layers of the pericarp are present […]."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "51ccb815-c7c1-418c-b857-d8fbd1cc58fc",
                                                "sense_id": "3c3a203a-5e70-3683-ab56-2292c98640a4",
                                                "text": "The pericarp of sorghums of the factorial titution BBSSrr, BBSsrr, BbsSrr, and BbSsrr may possess some t degree of coloration even though the pericarp color factor R is nt."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "1b1d8c2e-547c-45bf-bd6c-a8620e6fc2b8",
                                                "sense_id": "3c3a203a-5e70-3683-ab56-2292c98640a4",
                                                "text": "Many fruits show differentiation of the pericarp into layers – the plum, for example, has a thin outer pericarp forming the skin, a succulent middle pericarp forming the flesh and a stony inner pericarp surrounding the single seed."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "fbf3c055-10b5-4b35-b7da-033a49857a0b",
                                                "sense_id": "3c3a203a-5e70-3683-ab56-2292c98640a4",
                                                "text": "One important difference is the pericarp of sorghum is more bran-like and contains starch granules in the cells. However, as with maize, the sorghum pericarp is often referred to as a “hull” and is generally removed during processing."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "c5a48908-9961-3ed6-9f0a-f645d4a08d18",
                                        "entry_id": "53a6eb02-b29f-3fb4-a56c-117dc60d8176",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The outer layer of anything."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "figuratively",
                                            "rare"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "86432275-1c11-4038-abba-c70c578a01b8",
                                                "sense_id": "c5a48908-9961-3ed6-9f0a-f645d4a08d18",
                                                "text": "He learned, afternoon after afternoon, how to edge her into delicious frenzies, how to tremble the clitoris, pericarp, and tip."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    }
